<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thailand</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thailand is a country in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, historically home to a succession of kingdoms including Sukhothai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> note that Gupta religious art and iconography shaped artistic traditions in Thailand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> extended its influence over parts of present-day Thailand and contributed to the formation of later states such as Ayutthaya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thailand.jpg" alt="Thailand" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Thailand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Thonburi and Bangkok kingdoms consciously rebuilt Ayutthaya's model: Bangkok was laid out in imitation of the old capital, its laws and administration continued Ayutthaya's, and modern <b>Thailand</b> traces its history through Ayutthaya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…616–17, founded Isanapura, the site of Sambor Prei Kuk north of the Tonlé Sap, and left inscriptions there dated 13 September 627 and at Khau Noy in <b>Thailand</b> on 7 May 637; one calls him &quot;the King of Kings, who rules over Suvarnabhumi as far as the sea&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…forced the governor-general to admit its troops into the north in September 1940 and the south in 1941, and <b>Thailand</b> attacked in the Franco-Thai War of 1940–1941; the French navy won at Ko Chang in January 1941, but Japanese mediation gave Thailand parts of western Cambodia and Laos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Revire sees Funan as the catalyst for Dvāravatī in central <b>Thailand</b>, where similar artefacts occur, although Stephen Murphy warns that the ceramics of Nakhon Pathom show trade contact only.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Gupta artistic conventions in sculpture and iconography, seen at Sarnath and in the early cave temples of Udayagiri, were transmitted across Southeast Asia through trade and religious contact, shaping the artistic traditions of Cambodia, <b>Thailand</b>, and Java for centuries after the empire itself had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Khmer influence extended over present-day <b>Thailand</b>, Laos and southern Vietnam, shaping later states such as Ayutthaya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
